<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Early Cheras</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 300 CE (approx.) · capital Vanchi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sangam-age Chera kings of the south-west coast, who sold pepper to the Roman world through the port of Muziris.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> were one of the three crowned dynasties of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Cholas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka's</a> edicts in the 3rd century BCE name the Keralaputras among independent peoples beyond his empire. Their territory covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar</a> coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongu-nadu">Kongu</a> country inland; their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanchi">Vanchi</a> is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karur">Karur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> by most historians.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Chera</a> power rested on trade: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gems were shipped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> and other ports to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt</a>, as recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> and the Muziris <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patirruppattu">Pathitrupathu</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam</a> anthology, praises a succession of Chera kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imayavaramban-nedunceralatan">Imayavaramban Nedunceralatan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senguttuvan">Senguttuvan</a>, who also appears in the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silappatikaram">Silappatikaram</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a> inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pugalur">Pugalur</a> name kings of the Irumporai branch.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the 3rd century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> fade from view during the poorly documented age of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalabhra-dynasty">Kalabhra dynasty</a>. A later Chera state, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chera-perumals-of-makotai">Chera Perumals of Makotai</a>, arose on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> coast in the 9th century and claimed their legacy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/early-cheras.jpg" alt="Early Cheras" width="320" height="266"><figcaption>Image: Adapted from the maps by Y. Subbarayalu for Rajan Gurukkal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chera_country_(early_historic_south_India).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vanchi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Imayavaramban Nedunceralatan, Senguttuvan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tamil folk religion, Vedic religion, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sangam Cheras, Cheras of Vanchi, Chera dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=0&amp;focus=early-cheras" aria-label="Early Cheras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6781 1797 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Early Cheras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7101 2053L7198 2018"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7198 2018h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7101 2053h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7101 2053h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=0&amp;focus=early-cheras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

